  questionBlock("🟢", 7, "Walk me through what a well-designed membership program actually does for a med spa's revenue. Like, real numbers, what changes?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("A well-designed membership does three things to your revenue. First, it creates a predictable monthly baseline. If you've got 100 members at $199 a month, that's $19,900 coming in before you book a single appointment. Second, it increases lifetime value dramatically because members visit more frequently and they're far more likely to add on treatments. A membership client typically spends two to three times more annually than a non-member. Third, it lowers your cost to retain because you're no longer spending on reactivation campaigns for clients who already have skin in the game. The practices we work with that have fully installed membership programs see their monthly revenue stabilize in a way that makes everything else, the marketing, the staffing, the growth decisions, so much easier to manage."),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟢", 8, "Most med spa owners I talk to think a membership is just a loyalty card or a discount program. What are they getting wrong about what a membership is actually supposed to do?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("They're thinking about it as a perk when it's actually a retention infrastructure. A discount or a loyalty card is something you give a client to make them feel good in the moment. A membership is a commitment structure that changes behavior. When a client is paying monthly, they're psychologically invested. They show up for their appointments. They take your recommendations. They add on treatments because they're already in the mindset of investing in themselves. And from the practice side, a membership filters your client base toward people who are serious about their results, which means better outcomes, better reviews, and less chasing. The discount mentality actually attracts the wrong client. The membership model attracts the right one."),
