  sp(120),

  questionBlock("🟢", 11, "You've said that the front desk is one of the biggest revenue leaks in any med spa. What do you mean by that, and what does the wrong front desk conversation actually cost a practice?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("The front desk is the first and last revenue touchpoint every single day and most practices treat it like a scheduling function instead of a sales function. When someone calls to ask about pricing, the untrained front desk gives them the number and waits. No curiosity, no discovery, no invitation. That call ends and the lead is gone. Multiply that by every inquiry you get in a month and you start to see the real cost. We've done the math with clients and the conversion gap between an untrained and a trained front desk, just on inbound calls, can represent tens of thousands of dollars a year. The front desk isn't a receptionist position. It's a conversion position, and when you train it that way and give it the right script, everything changes."),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟢", 12, "What does a bad front desk script sound like versus a good one? Can you give me an example of how that one conversation can change whether someone books or not?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("A bad script sounds like an information transaction. Someone calls and asks how much Botox costs and the front desk says 'it starts at $12 a unit, most people need 20 to 40 units' and then just waits. That's a price quote, not a conversation. A good script sounds like a consultation. The front desk says something like, 'That's a great question, and honestly it really depends on what you're looking to address. Can I ask what's bringing you in?' Now you've started a discovery conversation. You've shown interest in the person, not just the transaction. From there a trained front desk can handle objections, create urgency around availability, and make a specific recommendation. The booking rate difference between those two approaches isn't small. We've seen it double in the first 30 days just from script training alone."),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟡", 13, "A lot of med spa owners hire for personality and assume the rest will work itself out. What's wrong with that approach when it comes to front desk performance?"),
  sp(160),

