  answerBlock("The difference is trust at scale and it translates directly into conversion. When someone's searching for a med spa in their area and they see one practice with 22 reviews and one with 210, they're not splitting that decision 50/50. They're going to the one with 200 almost every time because that volume signals safety. Your reviews are doing your selling before anyone ever picks up the phone. Practices with strong review profiles convert more of their website traffic, get more calls from Google Maps, and spend less per acquired client because organic trust is doing the heavy lifting that paid ads would otherwise have to do. The dollar gap between those two profiles compounds over time because the practice with 200 reviews keeps getting found, keeps converting, and keeps growing the gap."),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟢", 17, "Most med spas leave 60 to 75 percent of their potential reviews on the table. Why don't satisfied clients leave reviews, and what's the fix?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("Because asking for a review feels awkward, and most practices ask at the wrong moment or the wrong way. They'll put a sign on the counter, or someone will say at checkout, 'Feel free to leave us a review!' That's not a system. That's wishful thinking. Satisfied clients don't leave reviews by default because they're busy, they forget, and nobody made it easy or urgent. The fix is what we call the Reputation Engine: asking at the exact right moment within that dopamine peak window right after a treatment when the client feels incredible, and making the ask personal and specific. Not 'leave us a review' but a direct text with their name and a one-tap link. When you systematize that process and train your team to deliver it consistently, you can triple your review volume inside 90 days."),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟢", 18, "You talk about something called the dopamine peak window for asking for reviews. Can you explain what that is and why the timing of when you ask matters so much?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("The dopamine peak window is the short period right after a treatment when a client feels their absolute best. They just looked in the mirror, they love what they see, they're still in the energy of the experience. That's when their willingness to say something positive is at its highest. It's not 24 hours later when they're back in their routine and the moment has faded. It's not at checkout when they're thinking about their card and their next errand. It's in that warm window right after the treatment. When you train your team to make the review ask in that exact moment, with a personal direct link sent immediately to their phone, the yes rate is dramatically higher than any other approach. The timing isn't a small detail. It's the entire strategy."),
