  questionBlock("🔴", 19, "You managed three med spa locations and 44 employees at one point. What did burnout actually look like for you, and what finally broke?"),
  sp(160),
  questionBlock("🔴", 20, "A lot of med spa owners got into this industry because they love aesthetics or they love people. When did it stop being about that, and what does it feel like when the business is running you instead of the other way around?"),
  sp(160),

  questionBlock("🟢", 21, "What's the one thing that owners who are stuck always have in common, is there a pattern you keep seeing?"),
  sp(80),
  answerBlock("Every single time it's the same thing: they're the system. They're the one who knows all the clients, handles all the decisions, approves everything, fixes everything. There's no infrastructure running beneath them. So every time they try to step back, the business wobbles. And because it wobbles they step back in and the cycle continues. The owner becomes both the engine and the ceiling of the business at the same time. What breaks that pattern isn't working harder or even hiring more people. It's installing systems that run without the owner in the room: a follow-up sequence that fires automatically, a front desk trained to convert without escalating every call, a membership program that renews without a conversation, a marketing engine that runs consistently. When those pieces are in place, the owner can finally lead instead of just operate."),
